 ### Numerical Stability for Triangle FT
-Numerical Stability for Triangle FT handles numerical divergence when denominators in the triangle FT formula approach zero due to floating-point precision limits. It uses exact limit expressions for degenerate cases [S3, unofficial]. The steps are: 1. Calculate denominators $s, t, u$ (projections of edges onto frequency vector $\boldsymbol{\xi}$). 2. Check if any pair $(a,b)$ among $(s,t,u)$ satisfies $|a-b| < \epsilon$ (where $\epsilon$ is floating-point threshold). 3. If $|a-b| < \epsilon$, use the special case formula instead of the general formula [S3, unofficial]. The special case formula for $t=u$ is $g(t,t,u) = i \frac{e^{-it}}{t-u} + \frac{e^{-it}}{(t-u)^2} - \frac{e^{-iu}}{(t-u)^2}$ [S3, unofficial].
+Numerical Stability for Triangle FT handles numerical divergence when denominators in the triangle FT formula approach zero due to floating-point precision limits [S3, unofficial]. It uses exact limit expressions for degenerate cases [S3, unofficial]. The steps are: 1. Calculate denominators $s, t, u$ (projections of edges onto frequency vector $\boldsymbol{\xi}$) [S3, unofficial]. 2. Check if any pair $(a,b)$ among $(s,t,u)$ satisfies $|a-b| < \epsilon$ (where $\epsilon$ is floating-point threshold) [S3, unofficial]. 3. If $|a-b| < \epsilon$, use the special case formula instead of the general formula [S3, unofficial]. The special case formula for $t=u$ is $g(t,t,u) = i \frac{e^{-it}}{t-u} + \frac{e^{-it}}{(t-u)^2} - \frac{e^{-iu}}{(t-u)^2}$ [S3, unofficial].
 
